> Game engine: Unity 2D
Choice: Unity with the 2D toolset.
Unity fits your project well because the school allows Unity, WebGL, mobile, desktop, Phaser, Godot, etc., and expects a playable mini-platformer with powers, PNJs, alternative endings, architecture, prompt logs, and a build. Unity is a safe choice because it has many tutorials, AI can generate Unity/C# scripts decently, and WebGL export lets you share the game easily. 
Why Unity?
For your project, Unity gives you:
•	2D physics for platforming. 
•	Scene system for levels. 
•	Animator for cat/mouse/bird animations. 
•	Tilemaps for Metroidvania maps. 
•	WebGL export for school delivery. 
•	Huge community and documentation. 
•	Good compatibility with AI-generated C# scripts. 
Pros
•	Strong for 2D platformers
Unity has built-in tools for collisions, rigidbodies, tilemaps, animations, cameras, and UI. For a Metroidvania, that is basically your survival kit.
•	AI can help a lot
ChatGPT/Copilot/Cursor can generate Unity scripts like PlayerMovement, DialogueManager, PowerUnlockSystem, EnemyAI, etc.
•	Easy to show in demo
You can build for WebGL and let teachers launch it in a browser. No “please install 37 things and pray to the driver gods” moment.
•	Good for future portfolio
A Unity project looks solid on a student portfolio.
Cons
•	Unity can feel heavy
The editor is bigger and slower than something like Godot or Phaser.
•	Beginner learning curve
You will need to understand scenes, GameObjects, components, prefabs, scripts, inspectors, colliders, and animation states.
•	WebGL builds can be annoying
Sometimes WebGL has loading or performance issues, especially if the project gets too big.
•	C# scripts can become messy
If AI generates too much without structure, you can get spaghetti code with whiskers.

